# Text files keep LF in the working tree. The editor's own `files.eol` cannot
# repair a file nobody opened, and a global `core.autocrlf=true` on a development
# machine would otherwise leave files in the working tree with CRLF while their
# committed bytes are LF: a whole-file diff on the next commit, and `env: bash\r`
# in anything with a shebang.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Called out separately because a shebang is unforgiving: with CRLF the
# interpreter reads `env: bash\r`, is not found, and the script refuses to run on
# macOS and Linux. `*.sh` keeps this rule even if the one above is narrowed.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same discipline for hooks, which have no extension to match on. Git stores
# them as plain files, `core.autocrlf` converts them like any other, and the
# failure is silent: a CRLF hook is not executable and the guard it implements
# simply never runs. No `.githooks/` directory exists in this repository yet; the
# rule is here so that the day one is added it is already correct.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# The working archive is not in the tree any more, and `export-ignore` stays as the
# rule for the day one returns: `git archive` ignores `.gitignore`, so a directory
# kept beside the tree but out of the release needs an attribute to say so.
.hermes export-ignore
